Tests don’t protect code. They protect value. A test earns its keep only if it protects
customer, business, or developer value. If it doesn't reduce real risk, it consumes time
without improving outcomes. Lean also forces a harder question: what waste does this
test remove, and what waste does it create? Testing is often viewed narrowly as a primary
means of eliminating bugs, or in Lean terms, defects. They simply catch bugs
before users see them. Misaligned testing practices introduce other forms of waste.
¡ Overproduction appears when we generate more tests than the risk demands.
¡ Waiting shows up in long CI pipelines that block progress.
¡ Motion appears when developers context-switch during slow builds.
¡ Overprocessing creeps in when tests assert implementation details that refactors
legitimately change.

